nk of an  egalitarian  society either but virtue ethics really  is egalitarian  because it says anybody can practice  and develop the virtues and by the way  those people who are rich those people  who are powerful  they probably don't deserve the riches  or power that they have because they're  not actually  virtuous and so i think that this  actually is  quite right the the the notion that any  one of us  can improve our minds can develop the  you know uh the virtue of wisdom can can  develop  uh justice and enact justice in how we  relate ourselves to our you know  immediate surroundings the people that  we're engaged with  other people in many other places the  environment  pick whatever you want that all of us  can develop and practice  courage or fortitude that all of us can  you know do something in terms of  temperance i think they're right about  that  and so it's kind of an interesting point  to close  on when we think of the key ideas of  this work  i would say that that is one of the  underlying messages throughout the work  any one of us  could be like the people that they're  putting forth as examples  but we have to do the work  and put in the time and think things  through  in order to figure out what our role is  and  actually practice it  so i think there are a lot of good  points to this  this short book i'm going to just run  through them fairly quickly i think it's  very well thought  out kai and leo clearly put a lot of  time and discussion into what would be a  good  book to reach the ordinary person who  likes to read and maybe doesn't know  that much about stoicism  but also write a book that could be  interesting for those of us who've been  studying it for for quite a long time  and practicing it  so that's one good point i really think  that they do a great job  in highlighting the nature  the scope the meaning and the  application  of the virtues that's a really important  point and that connects it back to  classical stoicism i think  i